sections.s2_technical:
A compare-and-swap operation atomically says "if the current value equals the expected value, replace it with the new value, otherwise fail." The classes `AtomicReference` and `AtomicInteger` expose this as `compareAndSet(expected, new)`. The flaw is that equality is checked only against the present value.

```java
AtomicReference<Node> top = new AtomicReference<>(nodeA);
Node observed = top.get();                 // observes A
// ... thread is preempted here ...
// another thread: pop A, push B, pop B, push A  → top is A again
top.compareAndSet(observed, observed.next); // SUCCEEDS incorrectly! A==A
```

This matters most in lock-free stacks and free-lists built on linked nodes. A thread reads the top node A, is paused, and meanwhile the node A is popped, reused, and pushed back (common with object pooling). The paused thread's CAS against A succeeds, but the `next` pointer it is about to install is stale, corrupting the stack.

`AtomicStampedReference<V>` pairs the reference with an int stamp. You read both with `getReference()` and `getStamp()`, or together via `get(int[] stampHolder)`, and you update with `compareAndSet(expectedRef, newRef, expectedStamp, newStamp)`, which succeeds only if both the reference and the stamp still match. The convention is to increment the stamp on every successful update, turning the stamp into a monotonically increasing version number.

```java
AtomicStampedReference<Node> top = new AtomicStampedReference<>(nodeA, 0);
int[] stampHolder = new int[1];
Node observed = top.get(stampHolder);          // read ref + stamp together
int stamp = stampHolder[0];                    // e.g. 7
// ... even if value returns to A, the stamp advanced to 9 meanwhile ...
boolean ok = top.compareAndSet(observed, observed.next, stamp, stamp + 1);
// ok == false because the stamp no longer matches → ABA detected, retry
```

A related class is `AtomicMarkableReference`, which pairs the reference with a single boolean mark rather than an int stamp; it is used when you only need a one-bit flag, such as logically marking a node as deleted, rather than a full version counter.

A senior nuance: ABA is only a problem when the identity or history of the value matters, which is the case for pointer-based lock-free structures and object reuse. For a simple counter with AtomicInteger, ABA is usually harmless because you care only about the final number, not the path. Also, garbage-collected languages like Java reduce but do not eliminate ABA: because the GC will not reuse a node's memory while you hold a reference to it, pure pointer-recycling ABA is less common than in C, but it still occurs whenever you deliberately recycle objects (pools, free-lists) or when the logical state, not the memory address, round-trips. So the fix is to add a version stamp whenever your algorithm's correctness depends on "did anyone touch this in between," and AtomicStampedReference is the standard tool.

